<!-- Section 1: sec-intro -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Leg A From Projection Calculator</h2>
  <p>This calculator finds leg a of a right triangle when you know the full hypotenuse c and the projection p that belongs to leg a. You enter those two lengths, and it returns the actual side length of leg a, not a squared value and not a projection segment. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{c \\times p}]]')} />, where c is the complete hypotenuse and p is the segment of the hypotenuse next to leg a.</p>
  <p>For example, if <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 3.6]]')} /> cm, then <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{10 \\times 3.6} = \\sqrt{36} = 6]]')} /> cm. The product 36 is only an intermediate step. Taking the square root turns it back into a length, and that length is the side you are looking for.</p>
  <p>The calculator sits at the top of this page, followed by the formula, a labeled diagram, a proof of the projection theorem, worked examples, and checks you can run on any answer. The guide suits middle school and high school students, teachers, tutors, and parents helping with geometry homework.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: sec-calculate -->
<section id="sec-calculate" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Calculate Right Triangle Leg A From Projection</h2>
  <p>The calculator has two input fields. The first takes the hypotenuse c, the full side opposite the right angle. The second takes the projection p, the segment of the hypotenuse that belongs to leg a. Both lengths must use the same unit, so convert first if one measurement is in centimeters and the other is in meters.</p>
  <p>Projection p must be smaller than c. That rule is built into the geometry: p is only part of the hypotenuse, so it can never equal or exceed the whole side. If your p value is larger than c, you have probably entered a leg, the altitude, or the wrong projection.</p>
  <p>Click Calculate Leg a to see the result, and use Reset to clear both fields before a new problem. The result is a length in the unit you entered, so a hypotenuse of 10 cm and a projection of 3.6 cm return 6 cm, with no extra conversion step needed.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: sec-what-leg-a -->
<section id="sec-what-leg-a" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is Leg A in a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>A right triangle has two perpendicular sides, called legs, and one longest side, called the hypotenuse. The two legs meet at the right angle, and the hypotenuse sits opposite that angle. In this guide the triangle is ABC with the right angle at C, leg a is side AC, leg b is side BC, and the hypotenuse c is side AB.</p>
  <p>Leg a is one of the two sides that form the right angle, so it is a genuine side of the triangle. It is not a segment drawn inside the triangle, and it is not the projection p. The projection p lies flat along the hypotenuse, while leg a runs from vertex A to the right-angle vertex C. Keeping those two roles apart is the first step in using the formula correctly.</p>
  <p>Leg a differs from leg b only by position: leg a touches vertex A, and leg b touches vertex B. Each leg has its own projection, which is why the formula for a uses p while the formula for b uses q, a pairing fixed by the labeling.</p>
  <p>In any nondegenerate right triangle, leg a is shorter than the hypotenuse. That gives a quick sense check on every answer: if your calculated leg a comes out longer than c, something went wrong in the inputs or the arithmetic, and the diagram labels are the first place to look.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-what-projection-p -->
<section id="sec-what-projection-p" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is Projection P in a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>Draw the altitude from the right-angle vertex C perpendicular to the hypotenuse AB. It meets the hypotenuse at a point called the foot, labeled D. That point splits the hypotenuse into two adjacent segments, and those segments are the projections. Segment AD is projection p, and segment DB is projection q.</p>
  <p>Projection p belongs to leg a because it sits next to vertex A, the same vertex where leg a begins. In the small triangle ACD, leg a is the hypotenuse and p is one of its legs, which is the geometric link that makes the projection theorem work. Projection q, by the same reasoning, belongs to leg b, which touches vertex B.</p>
  <p>Projections are lengths along the hypotenuse, measured in the same unit as the other sides. They are not perpendicular to anything, and they are not sides of the original triangle. A projection is best pictured as the shadow a leg casts onto the hypotenuse when the altitude drops straight down to it.</p>
  <p>Because p and q are the two parts of one straight side, they add up to the whole hypotenuse:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = p + q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>That addition is useful in its own right. If you know c and one projection, you can find the other by subtraction, a step the later section on the other projection works through in full.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: sec-formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Leg A From Projection Formula</h2>
  <p>The projection theorem states that the square of leg a equals the hypotenuse multiplied by its projection:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 = c \\times p]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Taking the positive square root of both sides gives the working formula for this page:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\sqrt{c \\times p}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The calculation runs in two distinct stages. Stage 1 multiplies the hypotenuse by the projection, and the result is a squared length, not a side. Stage 2 takes the positive square root, which converts that squared length back into an ordinary length. Skipping stage 2 is the single most common error with this formula, and the next section explains why the square root cannot be left out.</p>
  <p>Units need care at both stages. If c and p are both in centimeters, their product is in square centimeters, and the square root returns centimeters. Mixing units, such as meters for c and centimeters for p, gives a meaningless product, so convert to one unit before multiplying. The positive root is used because a side length is always positive.</p>
  <p>A small numerical example shows both stages at work. With <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 3.6]]')} /> cm, stage 1 gives <span set:html={rich('[[10 \\times 3.6 = 36]]')} />, and stage 2 gives <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{36} = 6]]')} /> cm. The number 36 answers no question on its own. Only after the square root does it become the leg length.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('leg-a-projection-theorem.svg')} alt="Right triangle ABC with altitude CD, showing leg a squared equals the hypotenuse c multiplied by projection p" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The projection theorem: a squared equals c times p, so a equals the square root of c times p.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-why-sqrt -->
<section id="sec-why-sqrt" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Is There a Square Root in the Formula?</h2>
  <p>The square root is there because the multiplication produces a squared quantity. The theorem does not say that leg a equals the product of c and p. It says that the square of leg a equals that product. Undoing a square requires a square root, just as undoing a doubling requires halving.</p>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 3.6]]')} />. Their product is 36, which is <span set:html={rich('[[a^2]]')} />, the square of the leg. The leg itself is the number that multiplies by itself to give 36, which is 6. Writing <span set:html={rich('[[a = 36]]')} /> would claim a side of 36 units in a triangle whose hypotenuse is only 10, which is plainly impossible.</p>
  <p>The table below keeps the three quantities apart, since confusing them causes most mistakes with this formula.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Quantity</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Meaning</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Example with c = 10, p = 3.6</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">The leg length itself</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">6</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a squared</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">The leg length multiplied by itself</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">36</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">c times p</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">The product the theorem equates with a squared</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">36</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Square root of cp</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">The step that recovers a from a squared</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">6</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-how -->
<section id="sec-how" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Leg A Calculator</h2>
  <p>Ten short steps take you from a labeled diagram to a checked answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the right triangle in your problem and confirm it has a right angle.</li>
    <li>Find the altitude from the right angle and mark where it meets the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Identify the full hypotenuse c, the side opposite the right angle.</li>
    <li>Identify projection p, the segment of the hypotenuse next to vertex A, where leg a begins.</li>
    <li>Check that p is smaller than c. If it is not, relabel the diagram before continuing.</li>
    <li>Convert c and p to the same unit if they were given in different units.</li>
    <li>Enter c in the Hypotenuse c field.</li>
    <li>Enter p in the Projection p field. Do not enter q here, since that would return leg b instead.</li>
    <li>Click Calculate Leg a and read the result.</li>
    <li>Use Reset before starting a new problem.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The calculator does not accept q, so a problem giving only q needs one extra subtraction step, covered below. Entering q in the p field still produces a number, but it will be the wrong leg, so the label check in step 4 matters most.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-step-example -->
<section id="sec-step-example" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Example of Finding Leg A</h2>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 3.6]]')} /> cm. The four steps below show the full working, with nothing skipped.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula: a = sqrt(cp).</li>
    <li>Substitute the values: a = sqrt(10 x 3.6).</li>
    <li>Multiply inside the root: a = sqrt(36).</li>
    <li>Take the square root: a = 6 cm.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The answer 6 cm is the actual perpendicular side AC, not a projection and not an area. A short verification closes the loop: <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = 6^2 = 36]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c \\times p = 10 \\times 3.6 = 36]]')} />, so both sides of the theorem agree.</p>
  <p>For reference, the other projection is <span set:html={rich('[[q = 10 - 3.6 = 6.4]]')} /> cm, leg b is <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{10 \\times 6.4} = 8]]')} /> cm, and the altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\sqrt{3.6 \\times 6.4} = 4.8]]')} /> cm, confirming the familiar 6-8-10 right triangle.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('leg-a-example-c10-p36.svg')} alt="Right triangle with hypotenuse 10 cm and projection 3.6 cm, worked to give leg a equals 6 cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Worked example: c = 10 cm and p = 3.6 cm give a = sqrt(36) = 6 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

