  <h2>Right Triangle Leg A From Projection Diagram Explained</h2>
  <p>The main diagram shows triangle ABC with the right angle at C. Side AC is leg a, side BC is leg b, and side AB is the hypotenuse c. Altitude CD drops from C perpendicular to AB and touches it at D, and the right-angle marker at D confirms that CD is truly perpendicular.</p>
  <p>Point D splits the hypotenuse into two parts: segment AD is projection p and segment DB is projection q. Leg a and projection p are highlighted because they are the pair this page is about. Trace the diagram in this order to fix the labels: start at A, run along leg a to C, then along leg b to B, then back along the hypotenuse from B to A, pausing at D to see the split into q and p.</p>
  <p>The pairing of a with p becomes visible in the small triangle ACD, where leg a is the hypotenuse and p is the side lying along the big hypotenuse. Because triangle ACD is similar to triangle ABC, the ratio of p to a in the small triangle equals the ratio of a to c in the big one, which the proof section turns into <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = cp]]')} />.</p>
