<!-- Section 10: sec-proof -->
<section id="sec-proof" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Projection Theorem Work?</h2>
  <p>The proof rests on similar triangles. Drawing altitude CD divides triangle ABC into two smaller right triangles, ACD and CBD, and both are similar to the original triangle. Similarity means their corresponding sides stay in the same ratio, and one of those ratios gives the projection theorem directly.</p>
  <p>Focus on triangle ACD and the whole triangle ABC. Both contain the angle at A, and both contain a right angle, so their third angles must match as well. Matching the sides that sit in the same positions gives the proportion:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{AD}{AC} = \\frac{AC}{AB}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Now substitute the standard symbols: <span set:html={rich('[[AD = p]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[AC = a]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[AB = c]]')} />. The proportion becomes:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{p}{a} = \\frac{a}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Cross-multiplying clears the fractions. The left numerator times the right denominator equals the right numerator times the left denominator, so <span set:html={rich('[[p \\times c = a \\times a]]')} />, which is:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 = c \\times p]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Taking the positive square root of both sides finishes the proof, giving <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{c \\times p}]]')} />. The same argument applied to the other small triangle, CBD, gives <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = c \\times q]]')} />. Similarity does all the work: matching sides of similar triangles stay proportional.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('leg-a-similar-triangles.svg')} alt="Original right triangle ABC beside smaller similar triangles ACD and CBD, showing the proportion p over a equals a over c" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Similar-triangles proof: p over a equals a over c, so cross-multiplying gives a squared equals c times p.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Finding Leg A From Projection</h2>
  <p>Each example states the given values, the substitution, and the final answer. The commentary stays short so the arithmetic stays visible, and every example keeps its units consistent from start to finish.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Basic Whole Numbers</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 4]]')} /> cm, the formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{10 \\times 4} = \\sqrt{40}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[a \\approx 6.325]]')} /> cm. The product 40 is not a perfect square, so the answer stays as an approximation rounded to three decimal places. Note that 6.325 cm is shorter than the 10 cm hypotenuse, as it must be.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Perfect Square Result</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 3.6]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{10 \\times 3.6} = \\sqrt{36} = 6]]')} /> cm. When the product is a perfect square, the answer is exact, with no rounding needed. This is the same triangle as the step-by-step example, and its other leg is 8 cm.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Larger Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{25 \\times 9} = \\sqrt{225} = 15]]')} /> m. Larger numbers change nothing about the method: multiply, then take the root. The other projection here is 16 m, which pairs with a leg of 20 m, making the classic 15-20-25 triangle.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Decimal Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 12.5]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 3.2]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{12.5 \\times 3.2} = \\sqrt{40}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[a \\approx 6.325]]')} /> cm. This matches Example 1 in value because both products equal 40, a useful reminder that different inputs can share a product. Keep the full product inside the root until the final rounding step.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Equal Hypotenuse Projections</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 20]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 10]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{20 \\times 10} = \\sqrt{200} = 10\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm, so <span set:html={rich('[[a \\approx 14.142]]')} /> cm. Equal projections mean the altitude strikes the midpoint of the hypotenuse, which happens exactly when the two legs are equal. The triangle is therefore an isosceles right triangle, and leg b equals leg a.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Small Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 8]]')} /> mm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 2]]')} /> mm, <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{8 \\times 2} = \\sqrt{16} = 4]]')} /> mm. Small units work exactly like large ones, and the answer stays in millimeters because both inputs were in millimeters. Scaling a problem down never changes the steps.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Different Units</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 0.5]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 20]]')} /> cm, convert first: 0.5 m equals 50 cm. Then <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{50 \\times 20} = \\sqrt{1000}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[a \\approx 31.623]]')} /> cm. Multiplying 0.5 by 20 without converting would have given a root of 10, a wrong answer with no valid unit behind it.</p>
  <h3>Example 8: Fractional Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 16]]')} /> ft and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 4]]')} /> ft, <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{16 \\times 4} = \\sqrt{64} = 8]]')} /> ft. The product 64 is a perfect square, so the answer is exact. A quick sense check passes: 8 ft is half the hypotenuse, and the other leg works out to about 13.856 ft, which is longer than a but still shorter than 16 ft.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-other-projection -->
<section id="sec-other-projection" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find Leg A When the Other Projection Is Known</h2>
  <p>Some problems give q instead of p. The calculator on this page does not accept q, but that is only a one-step detour, not a dead end. Since the two projections add up to the hypotenuse, subtract to recover p first:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[p = c - q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} /> cm. Subtracting gives <span set:html={rich('[[p = 25 - 16 = 9]]')} /> cm. Now the ordinary formula applies: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{25 \\times 9} = \\sqrt{225} = 15]]')} /> cm. If you had entered 16 directly as p, the calculator would have returned 20 cm, which is leg b, not leg a.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/right-triangle-projection-q-calculator/">projection q calculator</a> can help when q itself needs finding, and the <a href="/right-triangle-projection-p-calculator/">projection p calculator</a> covers the reverse step. Either way, the rule stays the same: find p first, then use <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{c \\times p}]]')} />.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-relationship -->
<section id="sec-relationship" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Leg A, Hypotenuse, and Projection P</h2>
  <p>One theorem links three quantities, so rearranging it finds whichever one is missing. The three forms are:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = \\sqrt{c \\times p}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The table below shows each rearrangement, what it finds, and what must be known first.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Missing Quantity</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Formula</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Known Values Needed</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Leg a</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a = sqrt(cp)</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse c and projection p</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse c</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">c = a squared / p</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Leg a and projection p</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Projection p</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">p = a squared / c</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Leg a and hypotenuse c</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-leg-b -->
<section id="sec-leg-b" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Leg A and Leg B</h2>
  <p>Leg b follows the mirror image of the leg a formula. Where leg a pairs with projection p, leg b pairs with projection q:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = \\sqrt{c \\times q}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use one consistent triangle to see both legs together: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{25 \\times 9} = 15]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{25 \\times 16} = 20]]')} />. The two legs share the same hypotenuse but draw on different projections, which is why identifying p and q correctly decides which leg you get.</p>
  <p>The legs also constrain each other through the Pythagorean theorem, covered two sections below: knowing one leg and the hypotenuse determines the other. For problems that start from q rather than p, the <a href="/right-triangle-leg-b-from-projection-calculator/">leg b from projection calculator</a> runs the same method with the other pairing.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('leg-a-p-vs-q.svg')} alt="Right triangle comparing projection p paired with leg a against projection q paired with leg b, with c equals 25, p equals 9, and q equals 16" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Paired correctly: a uses p (sqrt(25 x 9) = 15) and b uses q (sqrt(25 x 16) = 20).</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-pythagoras -->
<section id="sec-pythagoras" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Projection P and the Pythagorean Theorem</h2>
  <p>The projection theorem and the Pythagorean theorem agree exactly, and the algebra shows why. Start from the two projection formulas:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 = c \\times p]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Add the matching formula for leg b, <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = c \\times q]]')} />, to get:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c \\times p + c \\times q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Factoring out c from the right side gives <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c(p + q)]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[p + q = c]]')} />, substitution yields the familiar result:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>A numerical check with the 9-16-25 triangle confirms it: <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = 225 + 400 = 625]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c^2 = 25^2 = 625]]')} />. The two theorems are not rivals. The projection theorem splits the Pythagorean relationship into one piece per leg, and adding the pieces rebuilds it.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify the Calculated Leg A</h2>
  <p>Three independent checks catch almost every error. Run at least one of them on every answer, and run all three when the problem matters.</p>
  <h3>Method 1: Projection Theorem Check</h3>
  <p>Square your answer and compare it with the product of c and p. For <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[a = 15]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = 225]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c \\times p = 225]]')} />, so the answer passes. If the two numbers differ, either the inputs or the square root step went wrong.</p>
  <h3>Method 2: Pythagorean Theorem Check</h3>
  <p>Find leg b from q, then check <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 15]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 20]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[225 + 400 = 625 = 25^2]]')} />. This check goes beyond the calculator, since it tests the whole triangle, not just the one formula.</p>
  <h3>Method 3: Size and Addition Checks</h3>
  <p>Confirm that p is smaller than c, that <span set:html={rich('[[p + q = c]]')} /> when q is known, and that leg a is shorter than the hypotenuse. In the example, 9 is smaller than 25, <span set:html={rich('[[9 + 16 = 25]]')} />, and 15 is shorter than 25. Any failure here points to a labeling error, not an arithmetic slip.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-properties -->
<section id="sec-properties" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Important Properties of Leg A From Projection</h2>
  <p>Eight properties follow directly from <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{c \\times p}]]')} /> and the geometry behind it. Each one doubles as a sense check on a new answer.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Leg a is always shorter than the hypotenuse in a nondegenerate right triangle, since p is only part of c.</li>
    <li>Projection p is always smaller than c, and it is always positive. Zero or negative projections describe no real triangle.</li>
    <li>Increasing p while keeping c fixed increases a, because the product inside the root grows.</li>
    <li>Increasing c while keeping p fixed increases a mathematically, provided the new values still describe a real triangle with p smaller than c.</li>
    <li>Swapping p and q changes which leg is calculated: p gives leg a and q gives leg b.</li>
    <li>Equal projections produce equal legs. With c = 20 and p = q = 10, both legs equal 10 times the square root of 2, about 14.142.</li>
    <li>The result is a length, not an area. The squared product is only an intermediate step, and the square root restores ordinary length units.</li>
    <li>Scaling all measurements by the same factor scales leg a by that factor. Doubling c and p doubles a, since the product quadruples and its root doubles.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>When the altitude lands exactly at the midpoint of the hypotenuse, the triangle is isosceles, and the two legs must match, as the diagram below shows.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('leg-a-equal-projections.svg')} alt="Isosceles right triangle with hypotenuse 20, equal projections p and q of 10, and legs a and b each equal to 10 root 2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Equal projections: c = 20 with p = q = 10 gives a = b = 10 root 2, about 14.142.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Finding Leg A From Projection</h2>
  <p>Each mistake below is followed by its correction. Most of them trace back to labeling, not arithmetic.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Forgetting the square root: reporting cp as the answer. With c = 10 and p = 3.6, the answer is 6 cm, not 36.</li>
    <li>Confusing a with a squared: 36 is a squared in that example, while 6 is the leg. Say which one you mean before writing a number down.</li>
    <li>Using projection q instead of p: entering q returns leg b. With c = 25 and q = 16, the result 20 is leg b, not leg a.</li>
    <li>Entering a leg in the hypotenuse field: the hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle and the longest side. A leg entered as c distorts every later step.</li>
    <li>Confusing the hypotenuse with a projection: c is the whole side, while p is only part of it. Check that p is smaller than c before calculating.</li>
    <li>Using p greater than or equal to c: no such triangle exists, since p is part of c. Recheck the diagram labels if this happens.</li>
    <li>Mixing centimeters and meters: convert first, as in Example 7, where 0.5 m became 50 cm before multiplying by 20 cm.</li>
    <li>Using negative measurements: lengths are positive, so a negative entry signals a data error, not a negative leg.</li>
    <li>Applying the formula to a non-right triangle: the projection theorem needs the altitude from a right angle. Without a right angle, there is no projection p in this sense.</li>
    <li>Rounding too early: rounding the product before taking the root shifts the answer. Keep full precision until the final step.</li>
    <li>Using the wrong side correspondence in the diagram: pairing p with b, or q with a, swaps the legs. Match p to the leg touching vertex A.</li>
    <li>Treating the altitude as a projection: the altitude h is perpendicular to the hypotenuse, not a piece of it, so it never belongs in the p field.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If an answer looks wrong, stop and relabel the diagram before recalculating. Most of the errors above vanish once p and q are clearly marked on the hypotenuse and everything else is set aside.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Applications of the Projection Theorem</h2>
  <p>The most direct use is geometry homework and classroom work, where projection problems test whether students can label a diagram and apply <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = c \\times p]]')} /> instead of reaching for the Pythagorean theorem by habit. Similar-triangle exercises use the same setup, since the proof itself is an exercise in matching corresponding sides.</p>
  <p>Technical drawing uses the theorem when a right triangle must be reconstructed from partial measurements: if a drawing gives the hypotenuse and the foot of the altitude, the missing leg follows without remeasuring the shape. Teachers also use the setup as a bridge lesson between segment addition and similarity.</p>
  <p>One condition limits every application: the required right-triangle geometry must actually exist. The theorem describes exact geometric relationships, not approximate field measurements, so it never replaces careful surveying or professional engineering measurement. Within its proper setting, though, it turns two known lengths into a third with a single multiplication and a square root.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 20: sec-practice -->
<section id="sec-practice" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practice Problems With Answers</h2>
  <p>Try all six problems on paper before reading the answers. None of the values repeat the worked examples, and the last problem combines several ideas from this guide.</p>
  <h3>Problem 1: Basic Whole Numbers</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 36]]')} /> cm and projection <span set:html={rich('[[p = 4]]')} /> cm. Find leg a.</p>
  <h3>Problem 2: Decimal Projection</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 26]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 6.5]]')} /> cm, find leg a.</p>
  <h3>Problem 3: Large Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 100]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 64]]')} /> cm, find leg a.</p>
  <h3>Problem 4: Unit Conversion</h3>
  <p>A hypotenuse measures <span set:html={rich('[[c = 1.2]]')} /> m and its projection p measures 30 cm. Find leg a in centimeters.</p>
  <h3>Problem 5: Find p From q and c First</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 65]]')} /> cm and projection <span set:html={rich('[[q = 49]]')} /> cm. Find leg a.</p>
  <h3>Problem 6: Multi-Step With Leg b and Pythagoras</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 50]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 18]]')} /> cm, find leg a, then find leg b and check the result with the Pythagorean theorem.</p>
  <h3>Practice Problem Answers</h3>
  <p>Answer 1: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{36 \\times 4} = \\sqrt{144} = 12]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 2: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{26 \\times 6.5} = \\sqrt{169} = 13]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 3: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{100 \\times 64} = \\sqrt{6400} = 80]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 4: Convert 1.2 m to 120 cm, then <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{120 \\times 30} = \\sqrt{3600} = 60]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 5: First <span set:html={rich('[[p = 65 - 49 = 16]]')} /> cm, then <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{65 \\times 16} = \\sqrt{1040} \\approx 32.249]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 6: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{50 \\times 18} = \\sqrt{900} = 30]]')} /> cm. The other projection is <span set:html={rich('[[q = 50 - 18 = 32]]')} /> cm, so <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{50 \\times 32} = \\sqrt{1600} = 40]]')} /> cm. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[30^2 + 40^2 = 900 + 1600 = 2500 = 50^2]]')} />.</p>
</section>

